  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 1108 PM MST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nSnow is ongoing across the Winter Storm Warning and Advisory \nareas as of late this evening. The northern Front Range zone, \nwhich includes Rocky Mountain NP, was upgraded to a Winter Storm \nWarning for Saturday. Looking at webcams, snow is already coming \ndown with pretty decent rates and the best lift is yet to come. \nLift will maximize in the early to mid morning to early afternoon\nas the upper level trough approaches and the left exit region of \nthe jet is over the area. At the same time, a strong cold front \nwill be moving through the area. Models continue to trend higher \nwith QPF or the mountains, and therefore snowfall amounts. As the\nprevious AFDs have mentioned, there is potential for snow squalls\nthis morning and afternoon (primarily along and following the \nfront). The best chance for snow squalls today will be in the \nmountains, but we can't rule one out on the plains. Even if the \nplains and foothills don't see a snow squall, there is still a \nchance for banded precipitation this afternoon and early evening.\nWhat will likely start as rain will transition to snow as \ntemperatures drop behind the front. Areas that see heavy snow \nbands will experience significant decreases in visiblities and \nhazardous driving conditions. \n\nHigh res models are showing an anticyclonic turning of the winds at \nthe surface to around 750 to 700mb near the southern foothills. This \nwill allow for decent upslope in the south metro, southern \nfoothills, and Palmer Divide area. Right now, we're keeping snow \naccumulations below Advisory criteria with models showing a low \nchance (<15%) this area exceeds the 4 inch Advisory threshold.\nBut, we still considered a Winter Weather Advisory for our \nsouthern foothills zone given the ingredients in place and the \npotential impacts if a heavy band does form there. For now though,\nwe've decided to hold off on the Winter Weather Advisory in this\narea. For the metro, we are expecting rain to transition to snow \nbehind the front, with the potential for some light accumulation \non grasses. Road temperatures will be fairly warm, so unless a \nvery heavy snow band develops over the area, roads should remain \nmostly clear on the plains. The main travel impact with any snow \nin the plains will be lowering visibilities. \n\nSnow will taper off in the evening as subsidence builds in behind \nthe departing shortwave. Temperatures will remain cooler on Sunday, \nwith highs in the 40s to near 50 across the plains. More zonal \nflow will set up on Monday, with a weak disturbance moving through\nthe flow aloft in the evening. We could see some light snow \nshowers in the mountains as the disturbance approaches and moves \nthrough, but impacts should remain minimal. \n\nOur next system will develop off the West Coast late Tuesday. An \nupper level low is forecast to move over southern California and \ntowards the four corners region Wednesday. The exact track of this \nsystem remains relatively uncertain and it will have a big impact on \nthe potential for precip and snow on the plains. If the track ends \nup trending more south, our snow chances would decrease \nsignificantly. But if the track of the upper low is closer to what \nthe current Euro is showing, we're looking at our next chance for\nmountains and plains snow late Wednesday through Thursday. The \ncurrent NBM has a medium chance (40%-60%) for at least a trace of \nsnow south of I-76 with this event and around a 20% to 50% chance \nfor at least an inch of snow for the metro and south. While \nthere's still a lot of uncertainty with the track, these are the \nbest probabilities for snow in the plains that I've seen so far \nthis season.",
